    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_72cf3d1c4405789431f9516e3f613eb68ed4cee13cbb826ed36698cb1eb77faf",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "For a fixed direction, the functional-equation factor first converts each nonzero critical-line intersection into a level crossing of a unit-circle phase; the logarithmic derivative then converts those crossings into accumulated winding, and integration yields the asymptotic directional sample count T/(2π) log(T/(2πe))+O(log T). Thus the geometric slice, phase-root parametrization, and sampling rate form one mechanism rather than three parallel descriptions.",
