    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_8fdd355c2c50636bcf2de98a992bf2966b437eb5ba90f09f40911bc8084e4108",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The directional count and second moment jointly force square-root-logarithmic magnitude in every sufficiently large dyadic interval: T log T samples cannot carry total squared mass of order T(log T)^2 if every sample is smaller. A separate first-moment step supplies the stated orientation, while the purely imaginary case instead uses the vanishing-mean estimate to obtain both signs. Normalizing the first moment into the mean circle is therefore distinct from, and more conditional than, the unconditional extreme-value mechanism.",
