    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_c76f37bb4febe9b720b9fc7903e45fa0bf6b7260c1eff2b53d490f938be68b30",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The arbitrary-direction phase coordinate has a classical specialization at φ=0: its level crossings are Gram points, and the accompanying real rotation turns ζ values there into Hardy Z values. Under that representation change, the general directional moment information becomes the Gram-point asymptotics Σ_{n≤N}Z(t_n)∼2N and Σ_{n≤N}Z(t_n)^2∼N log N, which then yields a fourth-moment lower bound by Cauchy–Schwarz without imposing a pointwise sign law.",
